{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>And the cows ate</b> — in my opinion this is a sign that the years of famine would consume the years of plenty, and from this Joseph learned to say to Pharaoh, \"And let them gather all the food of the good years\" and \"the food shall be for a storehouse for the land for the seven years of famine,\" for he saw that the good cows and the good ears would come into the inward part of the bad ones. And it is not advice, for the counselor to the king gave it to him; rather, in the interpretation of the dream he said thus: \"and all the plenty shall be forgotten,\" \"and the plenty shall not be known\" — this is the interpretation of \"and it was not known that they had come into their inward part, and their appearance was bad,\" for he saw that they were not healthy and good after eating them, but rather the good cows had served them as sustenance; had they not eaten them, they would have died of their wasting. And not according to Rashi's words, who said that \"and all the plenty shall be forgotten\" is the interpretation of the swallowing."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {0 "The word כַּחֲשֵׁיהֶן (their wasting/emaciation) is understood as the cows' physical deterioration from famine; rendered hyper-literally to preserve the anatomical force of the Hebrew."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:22:17.202728980Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}